														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4832796">
									

<p>TL;DR - I think that this idea is somewhat interesting and has the potential to become a good article, but I don't think it's quite at that point yet.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>As znaegele said in their feedback on your idea, at the stage that the idea is currently at I don't think there is enough to distinguish it from other similar articles (035 comes to mind). There are certainly articles that have been done regarding the alteration of the article itself (3939) and articles that include elements of disguising an anomaly's true nature (3651), but I will acknowledge that you have a unique combination here. It may be that a forum post is not enough to fully describe your idea, which is completely understandable, and if you have a sandbox page you could DM me on the IRC I would be happy to take a deeper look. As for specific points to work on, I think that some elaboration on the struggle between the host and the parasite as the article is being written could do a lot. How did the researcher acquire the parasite? Why is the researcher allowed to write the article when controlled by this parasite? When does the Foundation take notice? How is the article successfully written, or is it even able to be completed in the first place (artifacts of several different editors editing the page with or without the parasite could add some nuance)?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Definitely don't toss this idea out, as I think it has the potential to be great. Good luck with your writings! :)</p>

